until it reaches four residues from an (</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="1" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>α</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>1→ 6) branch point.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="110000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="0000FF"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Debranching enzyme</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t> transfers a block of three residues to the nonreducing end of the chain.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="110000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="0000FF"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Debranching enzyme</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t> cleaves the single remaining (</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="1" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>α</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>1→6)-linked glucose, which becomes a free glucose unit (i.e., NOT glucose-1-phosphate).</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="A diagram of glycogen breakdown near an (alpha 1 [right arrow]6) branch point. At the top, nonreducing ends start with 5 light blue hexagons all linked with a line and extending right. To the right of those are three yellow and one pink hexagon. Below that line of hexagons are 12 light blue hexagons, all with lines between them. The furthest right hexagon has three dashed lines extending right. These all represent glycogen, which has an arrow pointing down to glucose 1-phosphate molecules. In the middle of the arrow is glycogen phosphorylase (in blue). These molecules are represented by 5 light blue hexagons with black dots on the right point of the hexagon with a space, then three yellow and one pink hexagon, all connected with a line. Below that line are five more light blue hexagons with dots on their right points. These five are set just to the left of the ones above them. There is a space, then 7 light blue hexagons, all connected with lines. The furthest right hexagon has three dashed lines extending right. There is a line from the pink hexagon on the line above to the third from right light blue hexagon below it. These molecules have an arrow pointing down to more molecules. In the middle of that arrow is transferase activity of debranching enzyme (in blue). The molecules start with three yellow hexagons, followed by 7 light blue hexagons, which are all connected by a line. The third from last blue hexagon has a pink hexagon just above it and to the left, with a line connecting them. The furthest right hexagon has three dashed lines extending right. These molecules have an arrow pointing down, and to the left is (alpha 1 [right arrow]6) glucosidase activity of a debranching enzyme. This arrow also has another arrow shooting right, pointing to a pink hexagon, representing glucose. The arrow points to a set of molecules that begin with three yellow hexagons, followed by 7 light blue hexagons, all connected by a line. The furthest right hexagon has three dashed lines extending right. These molecules represent unbranched (alpha1 [right arrow]4) polymer; substrate for further phosphorylase action." id="421" name="Google Shape;421;p57"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="381000" y="895350"/><a:ext cx="3048000" cy="5962650"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>